# AICL Example: Social Media Platform
# Feed generation, content moderation, graph storage, notifications, and privacy controls

Goal
    Build a social media platform that generates personalized feeds using graph-based
    ranking, implements multi-layer content moderation with human-in-the-loop review,
    stores social graphs efficiently for billions of edges, delivers real-time
    notifications across multiple channels, and provides granular privacy controls
    allowing users to manage their data and visibility with precision.

Constraint
    Feed generation must complete within 200ms for up to 500 candidate posts
    Content moderation must process posts within 60 seconds of creation
    Social graph queries must resolve within 50ms for 2-hop traversals
    Notification delivery must occur within 3 seconds of triggering event
    Privacy rule evaluation must be applied before any content is served
    System must support at least 500 million active users with their social graphs

Risk
    Coordinated inauthentic behavior manipulates trending topics or amplifies misinformation
Recovery
    Deploy bot detection using behavioral fingerprinting; implement coordination detection
    via graph clustering; quarantine suspicious content pending review; reduce algorithmic
    amplification for flagged clusters; notify trust and safety team for investigation

Risk
    Content moderation backlog allows harmful content to remain visible too long
Recovery
    Auto-scale moderation workforce during spike events; implement severity-based triage
    with critical content escalated immediately; apply preemptive classification to hold
    suspect content until review; deploy enhanced AI classifiers during high-load periods

Risk
    Social graph database hot spots from celebrity or viral content cascading
Recovery
    Implement graph partitioning with virtual nodes for high-degree vertices; cache
    frequently traversed edges in memory; use read replicas for graph queries; apply
    rate limiting on fan-out operations for high-follower accounts

Risk
    Privacy rule misconfiguration exposes user data to unauthorized viewers
Recovery
    Implement privacy rule validation framework that tests all configurations against
    a canonical test suite; deploy privacy rule shadow evaluation before activation;
    audit privacy evaluations with differential privacy logging; provide data exposure
    notifications to affected users within 24 hours

Risk
    Notification storm overwhelms users during viral events
Recovery
    Implement notification batching and rate limiting per user; collapse similar
    notifications into digest summaries; apply priority scoring to deliver only
    high-relevance notifications in real-time; defer low-priority notifications
    to scheduled digest windows

Risk
    Feed ranking algorithm amplifies divisive or engagement-bait content
Recovery
    Implement content quality signals in ranking model; penalize clickbait patterns
    using headline-content disparity detection; boost diverse viewpoint exposure;
    run continuous fairness auditing on feed composition; provide user controls for
    feed diversity preferences

Layer
    SubLayer: FeedLayer
        Feed ranking, candidate generation, freshness management, diversity injection
    SubLayer: ModerationLayer
        AI classification, human review queue, policy enforcement, appeal processing
    SubLayer: GraphLayer
        Edge storage, graph traversal, community detection, influence scoring
    SubLayer: NotificationLayer
        Event detection, priority scoring, channel selection, delivery tracking
    SubLayer: PrivacyLayer
        Privacy rules, access control, data visibility, consent management
    SubLayer: InteractionLayer
        Like, comment, share, bookmark, reaction processing and aggregation

Validation
    Every served piece of content must pass privacy access check for the viewer
    Moderation decisions must be auditable with classifier confidence and human reviewer
    Social graph mutations must be ACID with follower/following counts remaining consistent
    Notification delivery must be idempotent and at-most-once per event per channel
    Feed ranking must include at least 15% diverse content sources per refresh
    User data exports must be complete and verifiable against stored records
    All content deletion requests must propagate to all storage systems within 30 days

Entity User
    userId: string
    username: string
    displayName: string
    bio: string
    followerCount: integer
    followingCount: integer
    postCount: integer
    privacyLevel: string
    accountStatus: string
    createdAt: datetime
    lastActive: datetime

Entity Post
    postId: string
    authorId: string
    content: string
    mediaAttachments: list
    visibility: string
    likeCount: integer
    commentCount: integer
    shareCount: integer
    createdAt: datetime
    moderationStatus: string
    editedAt: datetime

Entity SocialGraph
    userId: string
    followerIds: list
    followingIds: list
    blockIds: list
    muteIds: list
    closeFriendIds: list
    communityMemberships: list
    graphVersion: integer

Entity Notification
    notificationId: string
    recipientId: string
    type: string
    sourceUserId: string
    sourcePostId: string
    content: string
    priority: float
    channel: string
    readStatus: boolean
    createdAt: datetime

Entity PrivacyRule
    ruleId: string
    userId: string
    resourceType: string
    visibility: string
    allowedUserIds: list
    excludedUserIds: list
    customConditions: dict
    createdAt: datetime
    updatedAt: datetime

Entity ModerationDecision
    decisionId: string
    contentId: string
    contentType: string
    classifierResult: dict
    humanReviewer: string
    decision: string
    policyViolation: string
    confidence: float
    timestamp: datetime
    appealStatus: string

Entity FeedEntry
    entryId: string
    userId: string
    postId: string
    rankScore: float
    reasons: list
    freshnessScore: float
    diversityTag: string
    insertedAt: datetime

Behavior GenerateFeed
    Input:
        userId: string
        cursor: string
        pageSize: integer
    Output:
        feedEntries: list
        nextCursor: string
    Action:
        Retrieve user's social graph (following, interests, communities)
        Generate candidate pool from followed users, recommended content, and trending
        Apply privacy filtering to remove inaccessible content
        Score candidates using multi-factor ranking model
        Inject diversity: ensure representation from different sources and viewpoints
        Enforce freshness weighting for recent content
        Apply user-specific preference signals (engagement patterns, topic interests)
        Paginate results and return with ranking explanation metadata

Behavior ModerateContent
    Input:
        contentId: string
        contentType: string
        contentData: dict
    Output:
        moderationResult: dict
        escalationNeeded: boolean
    Action:
        Run content through multi-category AI classifier (hate, violence, spam, CSAM, etc.)
        Calculate classifier confidence scores per category
        If confidence above auto-action threshold, apply automated moderation
        If confidence below threshold but above review threshold, queue for human review
        If all categories below review threshold, approve content
        Check user moderation history for escalating penalties
        Record moderation decision with full audit trail
        Return result with action taken and appeal information

Behavior TraverseSocialGraph
    Input:
        userId: string
        depth: integer
        relationshipType: string
        filters: dict
    Output:
        connectedUsers: list
        graphMetrics: dict
    Action:
        Retrieve user's direct connections for specified relationship type
        If depth > 1, recursively expand to nth-degree connections
        Apply privacy filters at each traversal level
        Calculate graph metrics (centrality, clustering coefficient, bridge nodes)
        Respect rate limits and query complexity budgets
        Cache frequently accessed graph segments
        Return connected users with relationship metadata

Behavior DeliverNotification
    Input:
        recipientId: string
        type: string
        sourceUserId: string
        sourcePostId: string
    Output:
        notificationId: string
        deliveryStatus: dict
    Action:
        Evaluate notification priority based on type and recipient relationship
        Check user notification preferences and quiet hours
        Apply notification rate limiting per user per channel
        Collapse similar notifications if batch window is active
        Select optimal delivery channel (push, email, in-app, SMS)
        Generate notification content with personalization
        Track delivery status and read receipt
        Return notification ID with channel-specific delivery info

Behavior EvaluatePrivacy
    Input:
        viewerId: string
        resourceType: string
        resourceId: string
        ownerId: string
    Output:
        accessAllowed: boolean
        reason: string
        appliedRules: list
    Action:
        Retrieve privacy rules for resource owner and resource type
        Check viewer's relationship to owner (follower, mutual, blocked, etc.)
        Evaluate custom conditions (close friends, mentioned users, etc.)
        Apply block and mute lists
        Check resource-specific visibility settings
        Evaluate any time-limited or conditional access rules
        Log access evaluation for audit (with differential privacy)
        Return access decision with rule explanation

Behavior ProcessInteraction
    Input:
        userId: string
        postId: string
        interactionType: string
        payload: dict
    Output:
        result: dict
        notifications: list
    Action:
        Validate interaction is allowed (post exists, user not blocked, etc.)
        Check rate limits for interaction type per user
        Apply interaction (like, comment, share, bookmark, reaction)
        Update aggregate counters on post with optimistic concurrency
        Generate interaction event for feed ranking signal
        Trigger notification to post author if appropriate
        Update user engagement profile for recommendation signals
        Return interaction result with updated post metadata

Condition
    When post receives more than 10000 interactions per hour
    Then flag for enhanced moderation review, verify interaction authenticity,
         apply rate limiting to prevent counter manipulation, enable trending
         eligibility check

Condition
    When user blocks another user
    Then remove all social graph edges between users, hide each other's content,
         prevent future interactions, and cancel any pending notifications between them

Condition
    When content classifier confidence for harmful content exceeds 95%
    Then auto-remove content immediately, notify author with appeal option,
         queue for expedited human review, and log for policy enforcement audit

Condition
    When user's follower growth rate exceeds 500% of historical average
    Then flag account for potential inauthentic behavior review, temporarily
         reduce algorithmic amplification, and verify follower authenticity

Condition
    When notification queue for a user exceeds 50 pending notifications
    Then collapse similar notifications into digest, apply priority filtering,
         defer low-priority items to next digest window, and batch deliver

Event
    On PostCreated
    Action: Run moderation pipeline, update author's post count, trigger fan-out
            to follower feed generation queues, index for search, check trending eligibility

Event
    On ContentModerated
    Action: Apply moderation action (remove, label, restrict), notify content author,
            update moderation metrics, process any appeals, update classifier training data

Event
    On UserFollowed
    Action: Update social graph for both users, update follower/following counts,
            trigger feed recalculation for follower, send notification to followed user,
            update recommendation signals

Event
    On PrivacySettingChanged
    Action: Invalidate all cached privacy evaluations for user, re-evaluate visibility
            of user's content, update privacy rule cache, notify affected users if
            visibility decreased, log change for audit

Event
    On ViralContentDetected
    Action: Enable enhanced moderation monitoring, pre-cache content to CDN edges,
            prepare scaling event for feed service, update trending algorithm,
            notify content policy team for proactive review

Parallel
    Feed generation for millions of concurrent users
    Content moderation pipeline across all incoming posts
    Social graph traversal for feed candidate generation
    Notification delivery across multiple channels
    Privacy rule evaluation for every content access
    Interaction processing with counter aggregation

Optimize
    Target: Feed generation latency
    Priority: Candidate generation speed over ranking precision for first page;
              pre-compute feed candidates for active users; cache ranked feeds
              with incremental updates; use approximate ranking for scroll depth

Optimize
    Target: Content moderation throughput
    Priority: High-severity content processed first; AI classification runs in parallel
              with human review queuing; auto-action for clear-cut violations;
              batch processing for low-risk content during off-peak

Learn
    Target: Feed ranking personalization
    Goal: Maximize meaningful engagement (comments, shares) while minimizing passive scrolling
    Adapt
        Based: User interaction patterns, dwell time per content type, explicit
               feedback signals, and diversity satisfaction surveys

Learn
    Target: Content moderation classifier accuracy
    Goal: Achieve >95% recall for harmful content with <5% false positive rate
    Adapt
        Based: Human reviewer decisions as ground truth, appeal outcomes,
               emerging content patterns, and new policy enforcement requirements

Learn
    Target: Notification relevance scoring
    Goal: Achieve >60% notification open rate while minimizing notification fatigue
    Adapt
        Based: Notification open rates by type and time, user notification
               preference changes, unsubscribe events, and quiet hour patterns

Security
    Encrypt: User personal data, private messages, privacy rule configurations,
             notification content, moderation evidence packages
    Protect: Social graph access controls, feed ranking integrity, content
             moderation decision trails, privacy evaluation logs, notification
             delivery authentication

Security
    Encrypt: Cross-service data transfers, user data export payloads,
             moderator communication channels, appeal submission data
    Protect: Anti-scraping rate limiting, graph query complexity budgets,
             feed manipulation prevention, interaction authenticity verification,
             privacy rule tampering detection

Native Rust
{
use std::collections::{HashMap, HashSet};
use std::sync::Arc;
use tokio::sync::RwLock;

#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct SocialGraphEntry {
    pub user_id: String,
    pub followers: HashSet<String>,
    pub following: HashSet<String>,
    pub blocked: HashSet<String>,
    pub muted: HashSet<String>,
}

pub struct SocialGraphStore {
    entries: Arc<RwLock<HashMap<String, SocialGraphEntry>>>,
}

impl SocialGraphStore {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            entries: Arc::new(RwLock::new(HashMap::new())),
        }
    }

    pub async fn follow(&self, follower: &str, followee: &str) -> bool {
        let mut entries = self.entries.write().await;
        let follower_entry = entries.entry(follower.to_string())
            .or_insert_with(|| SocialGraphEntry {
                user_id: follower.to_string(),
                followers: HashSet::new(),
                following: HashSet::new(),
                blocked: HashSet::new(),
                muted: HashSet::new(),
            });
        follower_entry.following.insert(followee.to_string());

        let followee_entry = entries.entry(followee.to_string())
            .or_insert_with(|| SocialGraphEntry {
                user_id: followee.to_string(),
                followers: HashSet::new(),
                following: HashSet::new(),
                blocked: HashSet::new(),
                muted: HashSet::new(),
            });
        followee_entry.followers.insert(follower.to_string());
        true
    }

    pub async fn get_mutual_connections(
        &self,
        user_a: &str,
        user_b: &str,
    ) -> Vec<String> {
        let entries = self.entries.read().await;
        let a_following = entries.get(user_a)
            .map(|e| &e.following)
            .unwrap_or(&HashSet::new());
        let b_following = entries.get(user_b)
            .map(|e| &e.following)
            .unwrap_or(&HashSet::new());
        a_following.intersection(b_following).cloned().collect()
    }

    pub async fn can_view(&self, viewer: &str, owner: &str) -> bool {
        let entries = self.entries.read().await;
        if let Some(owner_entry) = entries.get(owner) {
            if owner_entry.blocked.contains(viewer) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        if let Some(viewer_entry) = entries.get(viewer) {
            if viewer_entry.blocked.contains(owner) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        true
    }
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct FeedEntry {
    pub post_id: String,
    pub rank_score: f64,
    pub diversity_tag: String,
    pub freshness: f64,
}

pub struct FeedGenerator {
    graph: Arc<SocialGraphStore>,
}

impl FeedGenerator {
    pub fn new(graph: Arc<SocialGraphStore>) -> Self {
        Self { graph }
    }
}
}
